/-!
# Separable field extensions are geometrically normal

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0C30,
lemma-separable-field-extension-geometrically-normal
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-separable-field-geometrically-normal).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Algebra

/-- A separable field extension remains normal after tensoring with any field extension.
Here separability means that every finitely generated intermediate field is separably generated;
neither extension is required to be algebraic or finitely generated.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0C30,
lemma-separable-field-extension-geometrically-normal
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-separable-field-geometrically-normal).

Proof:
1. Choose a perfect purely inseparable overfield P/k by
   lem-weil-pbc-perfect-purely-inseparable-overfield. Put R = K tensor_k P, and let q be 1
   in characteristic zero and the characteristic p otherwise.
2. For a nilpotent z in R, write z as a finite sum of pure tensors. Its K-coefficients
   generate a finitely generated intermediate field F/k, separably generated by the hypothesis.
   By lem-weil-pbc-separably-generated-tensor-reduced, F tensor_k P is reduced. Flatness of P
   over k (lem-weil-pbc-module-over-field-flat and def-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map) makes its map
   into R injective. Lift z to this tensor product; injectivity detects its nilpotence, hence
   its vanishing. Thus R is reduced.
3. Every finite tensor expression z has a q^n-th power in the image of K: choose n at least
   the purely inseparable exponents of all its P-coefficients. In characteristic p, iterated
   Frobenius additivity gives the assertion; in characteristic zero all coefficients lie in k.
4. A coordinate functional on a k-basis of P, normalized to send 1 to 1, induces a retraction
   of K into R by the tensor universal property. Thus R is nonzero. For nonzero z, its power
   z^(q^n) = c tensor 1 is nonzero by reducedness. Then c is nonzero, and
   (c^(-1) tensor 1) * z^(q^n - 1) is an inverse of z. Hence R is a field.
5. A field is normal: its only prime localization and fraction field are itself. Swap tensor
   factors to obtain normality of P tensor_k K, and apply
   lem-weil-pbc-geometrically-normal-perfect-closure-criterion. This gives normality of
   E tensor_k K for arbitrary E/k. Swap factors again. A ring isomorphism identifies primes,
   localizations, fraction fields and monic equations, so it preserves normality. -/
theorem normal_tensorProduct_of_isSeparableExtension
    (k : Type u) (K : Type v) (E : Type w)
    [Field k] [Field K] [Field E] [Algebra k K] [Algebra k E]
    (hK : IsSeparableExtension k K) :
    ∀ (p : Ideal (K ⊗[k] E)) [p.IsPrime],
      IsDomain (Localization.AtPrime p) ∧ IsIntegrallyClosed (Localization.AtPrime p) := by
  sorry

end Algebra
